# Contextual keywords are legal in binder and `local` BINDING positions, because they are ordinary
# names in every position that does not make them structural (spec/grammar.md:382). Those positions
# are just after `is` for all four, plus -- for `of` only -- the Heritage `value of ... for key`
# reader; a binder and a `local` name are neither. `empty` binds a `for ... in` loop variable,
# `member` binds a `for ... from ... to` counter, and `of` is bound with `local` and then assigned
# and read. None raises ol-reserved-word twice over: they are not built-in names at all, and under
# maintainer ruling #833 (issue #837) no BINDING position raises it for any name -- `local` is not
# one of the grammar's four declaration slots (spec/grammar.md:384,390). Every read resolves, so the
# checker reports nothing. The carve-out that makes these four special is proven at the DECLARATION
# slot instead, by contextual-keyword-as-procedure-name.
for empty in [1 2 3] [ print :empty ]
for member from 1 to 3 [ print :member ]
local of
:of = 5
print :of
